{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "12",
    "case_summary": "A 4-week-old male presented with left facial asymmetry while crying noted by the mother. The patient had previously passed infant hearing screening.\n\nLeft facial asymmetry observed during crying.\n\n- Imaging Studies:    * MRI findings: Enhancing mass in the left cerebellopontine angle and internal auditory canal    * Follow-up MRI (6 weeks later): Showed considerable growth of the mass    Image Title: Figure 1. Axial T1-Weighted Contrast-Enhanced MRI at 4 Weeks of Age    Image Description: Shows a mass at the Left Cerebellopontine Angle  - Auditory Tests:    * Auditory brainstem response (ABR) testing demonstrated profound hearing loss in left ear  - Histopathological Examination:    * Vascular lesion with low levels of mitotic activity    * Biphasic lesion composed of vascular nodules among spindle cells    * Glomeruloid-like architecture of vascular epithelial nodules    * Pattern similar to lymphangiomatosis    * Ki-67 labeling index: 5% to 10%    * CD34 and CD31: Positive for endothelial cells    * ERG stain: Identified nuclei of endothelial cells    * NF and S-100: Revealed attached cranial nerve",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Unilateral Cranial Nerve Deficit with Cerebellopontine Angle Mass and Profound Hearing Loss",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "supporting_facts": [
        "Left facial asymmetry while crying",
        "Enhancing mass in the left cerebellopontine angle and internal auditory canal",
